  % TODO: Latin Square Design
  \item (Exercise 13.3 from textbook) The \texttt{RepLS.csv} data file in Canvas contains data from a replicated Latin Square with four treatments; \textbf{row blocks were reused, but column blocks were not.} 
  
  \begin{enumerate}
    \item Write the ``standard model" for these data; clearly define which each term in the model represents, and the assumptions you are using. \textbf{Be very careful with the subscript notation for blocking factors}.
    \item Perform a hypothesis test for an overall treatment effect; state your conclusions and any evidence used to arrive at your conclusion.
    \item Test for treatment differences and use Tukey HSD with level 0.01 to analyze the pairwise treatment differences.
  \end{enumerate}
  

  % TODO: Random effects analysis
  \item \label{prob:bulls} (Exercise 11.2 from textbook) We wish to examine the average daily weight gain by calves sired by four bulls selected at random from a population of bulls. Bulls denoted A through D were mated with randomly selected cows. Average daily weight gain by the calves is given in Figure~\ref{fig:bulls}.
  
  \begin{figure}[ht]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{figure/bulls.png}
  \caption{\label{fig:bulls}Data for Problem~\ref{prob:bulls}, also available in the \texttt{bulls.csv} file in Canvas. This table is a screenshot from the table in Exercise 11.2 from Oehlert (2000).}
  \end{figure}
  
  \begin{enumerate}
    \item Write down a random effects model that could be used to analyze these data. \textbf{Be clear about your assumptions, and careful with subscripts}.
    
    \item Test the null hypothesis that there is no sire to sire variability in the response.
  \end{enumerate}
  

